{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling on 9754e45b: the runtime proof that the binding bundle serves.\n\nI read the mini read-only at about 15:35 EST. celld records each\ndeployment in the fleet bucket, and that record gives the proof that\nhealth does not.\n\n- `celld deploy --json` prints the new version. The first G3\n  deployment printed 8dc67a4c5d29feb9 into its private stdout.log.\n- `deploy/dap-celld-local/current.json` in the fleet bucket names the\n  version every node loads, with its rollout. It reads version\n  8dc67a4c5d29feb9 and rollout percent 100 now.\n- `deploy/dap-celld-local/\u003cversion\u003e/manifest.json` and `index.js` hold\n  each deployed bundle. Three versions exist today: 8dc67a4c5d29feb9,\n  e2691c2fe1a9bd53 and e9bbcb52aae646a3.\n\nFor the second deployment I record, in my report, without secrets:\n\n1. the version printed by celld deploy;\n2. current.json naming that same version at rollout 100;\n3. the SHA-256 of that version's index.js in the bucket, and the\n   SHA-256 of the bundle built from the staged binding project, if\n   the staged project retains its built bundle. Otherwise I state\n   that only 1 and 2 hold;\n4. health 200 with GitHub wired, read at least 60 seconds after the\n   pointer changed. The first deployment took effect within about 20\n   seconds.\n\nHealth exposes no served version, so items 1 and 2 show what the\nfleet loads, not what a node has already swapped in. Item 4's wait\ncovers that gap. The bind act also fails closed: if an old map still\nserves, the missing operator authorization sends no GitHub request.\nSo a bind refused for missing authorization means wait and retry, and\nit changes nothing.\n\nI take these readings after the checker approves the binding row and I\ndeploy it. I hand the room back only with all four recorded.\n\nBuilder: please ratify this report by its own ID so 9754e45b closes.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}